            <fr:title text="LC3 in Lean">LC3 in Lean</fr:title>
            <fr:taxon>Blog</fr:taxon>
          </fr:frontmatter>
          <fr:mainmatter>
            <html:p>
 I recently implemented a <fr:link href="https://github.com/dhsorens/LC3-Lean" type="external">formally verified virtual machine in Lean</fr:link> on which you can run 2048 in terminal! Try it out!
</html:p>
            <html:em>ISA-level model</html:em>
            <html:p>
The LC-3 is a small 16-bit instructional architecture: eight general-purpose registers, a program counter, condition flags, and a <fr:tex display="inline"><![CDATA[2^{16}]]></fr:tex>-word address space.
At each step the machine fetches a word from memory at the PC, decodes it as one of <html:strong>fourteen</html:strong> opcodes (from arithmetic and bitwise operations to loads, stores, branches, jumps, and subroutine calls), then updates registers, memory, and control flow accordingly.
Beyond those opcodes, LC-3 programs rely on <html:em>trap</html:em> routines for console I/O and on <html:em>memory-mapped I/O</html:em> for the keyboard—so a faithful simulator must implement both the core instructions and this I/O story. For more on the architecture, <fr:link href="https://www.jmeiners.com/lc3-vm/" type="external">see here</fr:link>.
</html:p>
            <html:p>
The code in <fr:link href="https://github.com/dhsorens/LC3-Lean" type="external">LC3-Lean</fr:link> is laid out as follows:
<html:ol><html:li>register and flag state lives in <html:code>LC3Lean/Registers.lean</html:code>,</html:li>
    <html:li>the flat memory model in <html:code>LC3Lean/Memory.lean</html:code>,</html:li>
    <html:li>opcode decoding in <html:code>LC3Lean/Instructions.lean</html:code>,</html:li>
    <html:li>the fetch–decode–execute step and per-opcode semantics in <html:code>LC3Lean/Execution.lean</html:code>,</html:li>
    <html:li>
    The runnable binary wires together loading of <html:code>.obj</html:code> files, the execution loop, and MMIO in <html:code>Main.lean</html:code>. If you open those modules, every informal claim in this paragraph—word size, register count, opcode coverage, traps, MMIO—is meant to be visible as definitions and executable code rather than hand-waving.
    </html:li></html:ol>
   The runnable binary wires together loading of <html:code>.obj</html:code> files, the execution loop, and MMIO in <html:code>Main.lean</html:code>.
</html:p>
            <html:p>
    For full details, <fr:link href="/dhsorens-0029/" title="The ISA-level details of LC3-Lean" uri="https://derekhsorensen.com/dhsorens-0029/" display-uri="dhsorens-0029" type="local">see this page</fr:link>.
</html:p>
            <html:em>Formal proofs</html:em>
            <html:p>
"Formally verified" here means that Lean checks mathematical theorems relating the implementation above to an explicit ISA-level specification of how each instruction may read and write registers and memory.
Those theorems are stated and proved in the <html:code>LC3Correct</html:code> package—especially <html:code>LC3Correct/ExecutionCorrect.lean</html:code>, with supporting lemmas about registers and memory in <html:code>LC3Correct/RegisterLemmas.lean</html:code> and <html:code>LC3Correct/MemoryLemmas.lean</html:code>. Together they aim to capture, opcode by opcode, that the Lean executor’s effect on machine state matches the spec encoded alongside it. All proofs pass without <html:code>sorry</html:code>!
</html:p>
            <html:em>Trust Base and Verification Boundary</html:em>
            <html:p>
The boundary worth keeping in mind is the thin C shim (<html:code>c/terminal.c</html:code>) used for POSIX terminal raw mode and polling: it affects how characters reach your screen and keyboard, but the LC-3 machine semantics and the correctness proofs live on the Lean side. Readers who want the precise logical statement for a single opcode should start from <html:code>LC3Correct/ExecutionCorrect.lean</html:code> and follow the definitions it references—the prose here is only a map to those lemmas.
</html:p>
            <html:p>
    For full details, <fr:link href="/dhsorens-002A/" title="Details on the Proofs of Correctness for LC3-Lean" uri="https://derekhsorensen.com/dhsorens-002A/" display-uri="dhsorens-002A" type="local">see this page</fr:link>.
</html:p>
